## What it does

The auth cartridge, `packages/auth`, is a package for one job with its implementation and required infrastructure.
It runs Better Auth, a library for sign-in and sessions, through `@alchemy.run/better-auth`.
It uses the implementation chosen by the [database](/systems/database) cartridge.
People sign in with email and password under `/auth`.

RPC means remote procedure call: a client calls a named operation on the server.
`CurrentPersonMiddleware` checks each call's session before it runs and provides `CurrentPerson`, the signed-in person's id.
This check is middleware: code that runs around a call.

A capability is one named action with shared value definitions and a server-side implementation.
`@rat-stack/auth/devtools` adds `runAsPerson`, the dev-only `rat_test_person` capability, and in-memory test people.

ratstack.sh has no sign-in. Today auth runs only in the web app's dev server, where devtools calls run as test people.

## The standard

- An RPC call without a valid session fails as `Unauthenticated`. That is the only auth failure sent to the client.
- A session whose user id fails the person-id schema check also fails as `Unauthenticated`.
- Test people and `rat_test_person` never reach production. Lint keeps `@rat-stack/auth/devtools` inside dev folders, the CLI, and tests (`rat-stack-boundaries/no-devtools-in-production`).
- A production build of the web app contains no dev module, no devtools code, and no test person.

## How to check

- `pnpm --filter @rat-stack/auth test` signs up, signs in, reads a session, protects an RPC call, and runs devtools calls as a test person.
- `pnpm --filter @rat-stack/web exec vitest run test/production-bundle.test.ts` builds the web app for production and checks the bundle.
